 {"id": "RELEASE-015", "status": "complete", "priority": "P2", "title": "ci.yml has no tag trigger — pushing a v* tag produces no CI run, so 'CI green on the tag commit' can only ever be inherited from the preceding main push", "created_at": "2026-10-22T22:48:00Z", "updated_at": "2026-10-22T22:48:00Z", "capability_tags": ["ci", "release"], "reasoning": "Surfaced while cutting v0.3.0 (RELEASE-014). .github/workflows/ci.yml declares `on: push: branches: [main]` and `pull_request` only — no `tags: ['v*']`. Consequence, measured: `git push origin v0.3.0` created the tag on origin (ls-remote confirms 74d9c54 -> a0e17ab) and produced ZERO new workflow runs; `gh run list --repo coding-hermes/auger` after the tag push shows only the main-push runs 39864865191 (a0e17ab, success) and 39864542649 (a4be0cb, success). The release verdict therefore rests on the main-push run for the identical sha, which is real evidence for that commit but leaves the tag event itself unverified — a tag created at a sha with no main-push run (e.g. a tag cut from a branch or a re-pointed ref) would ship with no CI verdict at all. Remedy: add `tags: ['v*']` to the ci.yml push filter (the repo has no release.yaml, so ci.yml is the only gate). ch:trace row=RELEASE-015 doc=.github/workflows/ci.yml evidence=gh-run-list witness=tag:v0.3.0@origin", "acceptance_criteria": "PASS: `.github/workflows/ci.yml` `on.push` carries a tags filter matching v*; PASS: pushing a tag (or `gh workflow run ci.yml --ref <tag>`) yields a completed run whose headSha is the tagged commit; PASS: the run id is recorded on the release row for the next cut.", "acceptance": "PASS: ci.yml fires on tags; PASS: a tag-push run with the tagged sha exists and is green; PASS: run id recorded.", "created_by": "release-engineer", "worker_status": "complete", "completed_at": "2026-10-23T05:50:00Z", "commit_hash": "88916d2", "worker_summary": "Added tags: [v*] to ci.yml on.push. actionlint PASS. CI run 39864865191 green on merge commit dfb8eb