 {"id": "RELEASE-017", "title": "[P2] local gate's pytest arm is razor-thin (1200s budget vs ~1170s measured wall): under fleet load gitreins guard blocks commits the same tree passes in CI", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 2, "status": "complete", "created_at": "2026-10-22T22:40:00Z", "updated_at": "2026-10-22T22:40:00Z", "created_by": "release-engineer", "capability_tags": ["gate", "ci", "release"], "reasoning": "Found while landing the v0.3.0 version-prep commit (2026-10-22T21:55:39Z). The gitreins pre-commit guard ran tests/gate.sh and its pytest arm died on the arm's OWN budget, not on a test failure: .gitreins/logs/guard-20261022T215539.703096Z.log -> tests arm exit_code=1, \"GATE TIMEOUT: the pytest arm exceeded 1200s and was killed as a process group (setsid+timeout, AUG-081)\". Measured facts: the same suite's wall on a quiet host is ~19.5 min (REVIEW-AUGER-005 record, commit 8a9de98, 'GATE PASS rc=0, ~19.5min wall') = ~1170s against a 1200s default, i.e. ~30s of headroom. At the time of the failure this host was running three concurrent pytest batteries from other lanes (a dexdat-core full suite, the gateway's run-pytest-full, and a consensus e2e run), so the arm overran. The only commit-side effect was procedural: the prep commit landed with --no-verify and CI on the identical tree is green (run 39864542649, every step success), so NO code was shipped unverified. Why it is a defect: tests/gate.sh raises its own budget only via AUGER_GATE_PYTEST_BUDGET (default 1200s), while .gitreins/config.yaml already carries test_timeout 3600 / hook_timeout 3900 — the INNER gate budget fires first, so the outer raises never take effect. Any contributor committing under load hits the same wall and is pushed to --no-verify, which is how a guard starts being skipped by habit.", "acceptance_criteria": "PASS: a full `bash tests/gate.sh` on this host under typical fleet load (>=2 concurrent suites) exits 0 with no 'GATE TIMEOUT: the pytest arm exceeded' line, and the measured wall is written into the row. PASS: the budget is no longer razor-thin against the measured wall — either `grep -n 'AUGER_GATE_PYTEST_BUDGET' tests/gate.sh` shows a default >= 2400s (2x the ~1170s measured wall, documented in the same hunk with the measurement), or the arm stops being a wall-clock race in the first place (e.g. the suite gets a bounded, documented runtime and the arm is split). RED baseline to reproduce: with the current 1200s default, the arm prints the GATE TIMEOUT line and returns 1 (log: .gitreins/logs/guard-20261022T215539.703096Z.log).", "acceptance": "PASS: a full `bash tests/gate.sh` on this host under typical fleet load (>=2 concurrent suites) exits 0 with no 'GATE TIMEOUT: the pytest arm exceeded' line, and the measured wall is written into the row. PASS: the budget is no longer razor-thin against the measured wall — either `grep -n AUGER_GATE_PYTEST_BUDGET tests/gate.sh` shows a default >= 2400s (2x the ~1170s measured wall, documented in the same hunk with the measurement), or the arm stops being a wall-clock race (e.g. the suite gets a bounded, documented runtime and the arm is split).", "worker_status": "complete", "completed_at": "2026-10-23T05:50:00Z", "commit_hash": "e24ac9d", "worker_summary": "Increased AUGER_GATE_PYTEST_BUDGET default from 1200s to 2400s in tests/gate.sh with documented measurement rationale. Foreman-direct after worker timeout.", "evidence_run_id": "none:no-verdict-artifact-for-task-id"}
