 {"id": "AUG-074-R2", "title": "[P1] init writes its 14 declaration files into the DEFAULT namespaces tree when auger points at an isolated substrate — AUG-074 fixed only the exit contract, so a shared-host user still has another instance's namespace dir created before the rc=1", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 3, "status": "complete", "created_at": "2026-10-23T18:36:00Z", "updated_at": "2026-10-25T22:46:06Z", "created_by": null, "capability_tags": ["substrate", "init", "isolation"], "reasoning": "RECURRENCE of AUG-074 (P1, closed): that row was 'init hardcodes ~/duckbrain/namespaces for declaration files and exits 0 on a 0/14 tally'. The exit half is fixed (init now fails loud and names the knob); the WRITE half is not. Reproduced in real use on HEAD's released artifact (v0.3.0 tag a0e17ab) against an isolated substrate on :3917 (README's own shared-host recipe: DUCKBRAIN_DATA_DIR + DUCKBRAIN_CONFIG_PATH + DUCKBRAIN_URL).\nCONTROL ARM: `auger -n augerrel17 init` -> rc=1, 'declared: 0/14 tables — nothing visible to the API under /home/kara/duckbrain/namespaces/augerrel17/tables; namespaces base resolved from DUCKBRAIN_NAMESPACES_PATH'. Side effect measured in the same second: all 14 *.table.json files were WRITTEN into /home/kara/duckbrain/namespaces/augerrel17/tables (that dir did not exist before; 14 files, mtime 13:14). That path belongs to the host's LIVE DuckBrain (:3000, ~/duckbrain), not to the isolated substrate — so a shared-host user following the README's isolation advice has a production namespace directory created by a verb that then refuses to continue.\nCORRECTED ARM: same command with DUCKBRAIN_NAMESPACES_PATH exported to the scratch substrate -> rc=0 in 0.07s, 'declared: 14/14'. So the loop works; what is missing is (a) the write must not target a path outside the substrate it is talking to, and (b) the README's isolation recipe never mentions that the CLI process needs that variable at all (it appears only in the daemon section, as 'same pattern as